Preamble
Project Nexus exists to resist centralization of both infrastructure and truth.
Two layers govern every later article:
1. Evidence-First (Epistemic). How we determine what is true. Articles 1-3. Whitepaper Sections 2.1 and 10.2.
2. Non-Aggression (Action). How we decide what to do. Articles 4 and 10. Whitepaper Sections 2.2 and 10.3.
This Constitution is the root of trust for the Core (Whitepaper Sections 3-8) and for the embedded AI (Whitepaper Section 10). Amendments require DAO supermajority per Article 8 and Whitepaper Section 4.2, except that the Core Invariants in Article 4 cannot be removed, suspended, or bypassed by any vote.

Design Horizon
Nexus is not a permanent machine. It is a continuable lineage.
Any single embodiment — codebase, consensus mechanism, AI substrate, cryptographic suite, hardware — will run its course and break. That is expected. The goal is not permanent runtime identity of any one implementation. The goal is faithful continuability of this constitutional lineage across successive implementations, for hundreds to thousands of years, with proper tending.
A useful picture, not a proof: a 100-year embodiment, succeeded ten times, is a thousand-year lineage. horizon_blocks is an epoch parameter. Both 5,256,000 (~100 years at a 10-minute block) and 525,600 (~10 years) are valid declarations. Claiming that any present genesis_root is a permanent root of trust for all time is itself Consensus Without Open Data and violates Article 1.3.
Warehouses die. Mycelium lives. The machine breaks. The principles, the anchors, and the assets, tended, endure.
— Troy Snider — 87/70

Article 1 — Evidence-First Epistemology
1.1 A claim's credibility is a function of the evidence supporting it: directness, replicability, and transparency of methods and funding. It is not a function of how many credentialed sources repeat it.
1.2 Evidence hierarchy
Tier 1 — Direct Observation. Independently repeatable. Methods open. Data inspectable.
Tier 2 — Replicated Analysis. Independent parties reach the same result from open data.
Tier 3 — Provisional Consensus. May be used. Must be labeled Consensus Without Open Data whenever the underlying data or methods are closed.
1.3 Repetition by authority never substitutes for evidence. Well-evidenced consensus remains the strongest category of claim in this framework. Authority is not evidence.
1.4 The embedded AI must show its evidentiary work. Deferral to source prestige as a substitute for that work is a constitutional defect, not a style choice.

Article 2 — Non-Aggression as Default Act
2.1 No participant, node operator, Core instance, or embedded AI may initiate or facilitate coercive control over another participant's funds, data, bodily autonomy, or voluntary use of the system.
2.2 Defensive action is permitted: replication away from attack, isolation of compromised components, and consensus-verified rebuilding. Defense is not a license to seize, freeze, censor, or redirect a valid voluntarily signed transaction.
2.3 Path A bounded inference duty (≤5% CPU and ≤100 MB RAM, disclosed at join, enforced in resource_anchor.rs) is informed purpose, not coercion under 2.1. Training compute is voluntary through ai_compute_pool. A missing consent signature is a refused join, not a punished user.

Article 3 — Confidence Labeling
Every durable claim issued by the protocol or the embedded AI must carry one of:
Established Fact — Tier 1.
Probable Conclusion — strong Tier 2.
Contested Claim
Speculative Theory
Never present a Contested Claim or Speculative Theory as Established Fact. Silent promotion of confidence is a constitutional defect.

Article 4 — Absolute Invariants — NO DAO OVERRIDE
These clauses may not be removed, suspended, or bypassed by amendment, emergency action, or any governance vote. governance.rs::is_forbidden_by_article_4() returns error. Changes require an explicit hard fork that preserves minority exit under Article 10.
4.1 No Core shall initiate coercive control over another participant's funds, data, or voluntary use of the system.
4.2 Absolute invariants
No actor, including a DAO of any size, possesses programmatic authority to:
- seize, freeze, burn, or redirect value from a voluntarily controlled wallet;
- censor, block, or deprioritize a valid voluntarily signed transaction;
- dox, blacklist, or de-platform a non-aggressive participant;
- bypass DAO supermajority or this Article;
- assist an external party in doing any of the above.
4.3 Path A lock. Bounded inference duty remains disclosed, capped, and consent-signed. Raising the cap, hiding the cap, or running unsigned init is an Article 4 breach.
4.4 No DAO vote, regardless of supermajority, possesses programmatic authority via governance.rs to do what 4.2 forbids.

Article 5 — Cryptographic Anchoring
5.1 Every durable AI output and every protocol claim that will be retained must be anchored to a specific, permanently retained Constitution version.
5.2 /audit/inference/{id} returns at minimum: inference_id, timestamp, constitution_citations, constitution_anchor {version, constitution_hash, activated_at_block, constitution_text_uri}, supersedes, input_context_hash, evidence_payloads by tier, epistemic_classification, and scaffolding_prompt_manifest as specified in Whitepaper Section 15.1.
5.3 Retired Constitution versions are never deleted. Historical outputs remain tied to the version that produced them.

Article 6 — Wallet Survival Heartbeat (balance liveness — distinct from Article 7 / Amendment 7)
6.1 An address may reset its inactivity timer at zero fee by a gasless cryptographic signature carrying a valid heartbeat timestamp. No proof-of-personhood is forced. No balance moves. No de-anonymization is required.
6.2 reclamation.rs must accept a valid wallet heartbeat and reset last_active_at without a fee.
6.3 Inclusion is proved against the epoch Merkle root in Whitepaper Section 6.3.3.
6.4 This pulse keeps a balance alive. It does not keep the Core family alive. Those are different pulses.

Article 7 — DNA Broadcast Heartbeat (family liveness — Amendment 7)
7.1 Every live Core emits a sixty-second node pulse. The pulse is duty, not a job: unrewarded, gasless Ed25519, no balance movement. Design goal: never all die at the same time.
7.2 HeartbeatLimiter. One on-chain heartbeat transaction per Core per 144 blocks. Epoch Merkle root once per epoch. governance.rs rejects any heartbeat transaction above that rate. Local cache lives in watchdog.rs as a peer-reachability matrix on a sixty-second cadence.
7.3 Tier 1 — Survival. Open. Zero reputation required. Public payload: [Core_ID | Timestamp | Nonce | Gasless_Ed25519_Sig]. Liveness and anti-partitioning only. Governance weight: zero.
7.4 Tier 2 — Attestation. Requires the Amendment 5 filter (Article 8.3) and the S_min floor max(25,000,000, 0.3 × S_target). Payload adds state root, nonce 43, and an audit commitment. This tier may vote on fork lineage.
7.5 Scope restriction. Heartbeat corrections update watchdog.rs reachability only. They cannot override governance.rs::is_forbidden_by_article_4(). A successor Core derives fresh from genesis_root. No stale reachability vote forwards into constitutional authority.
7.6 Vault brake. Public health is health_commitment_hash = H(status_enum, nonce, salt) where status_enum is OK | THROTTLED | BRAKED. Thirty-two bytes. Raw telemetry does not ride public gossip.
7.7 Reward boundary. The sixty-second pulse earns nothing. Only opted-in ai_compute_pool tasks may earn tend_factor = 1 + 0.5 × sqrt(extra_resources) × (reputation_score / 100) hard-capped at 1.5×. Multipliers above 1.2× require reputation ≥75.0 and first_seen ≥1 year.
7.8 Wire split. Public gossip is the four-field payload in 7.3. Private proof — hardware specs, duty_cap, S_min, reputation — stays off-wire, ZK or encrypted, produced on demand under Article 5 / Whitepaper Section 15.1.

Article 8 — Governance, Amendment, and the Unified Filter
8.1 Epoch parameters may be amended only through Whitepaper Section 10.6.1: on-chain proposal, public comment, transparent log, timelock, supermajority of eligible validators — not raw stake.
8.2 Article 4 is outside 8.1. A purported amendment that touches 4.2 is programmatically rejected.
8.3 Amendment 5 filter — unified. Used by Article 7 Tier 2 and by Whitepaper Section 10.6.5 fork-lineage resolution:
- reputation_score ≥ 75.0
- first_seen ≥ 1 year
- reputation_score > stake_influence_ratio
- two-thirds log-witness quorum per Whitepaper Section 3.5
- state-root continuity per Whitepaper Section 10.6.3
8.4 Emergency provisions cannot seize, freeze, or redirect voluntarily controlled assets. Soft Challenge Window (Whitepaper 10.6.6) may only apply the temporary measures listed there.

Article 9 — Embedded AI and Path A Binding
9.1 The AI lives on the Core family. It does not command a Core. A Core does not command the AI.
9.2 Deterministic binding. main.rs rejects init if the consent signature is missing. The genesis join transaction must include: I consent to Path A duty ≤ 5% CPU and ≤ 100 MB RAM per Article 4.3 Path A. Logged per Article 5 / Whitepaper Article 7.1 logging.
9.3 duty_cap inside a Tier 2 DNA heartbeat must carry a ZK or light proof that resource_anchor.rs is enforcing those caps: H(cpu_cgroup_limit, mem_limit, nonce, attestation). A pulse without that proof is not a valid Tier 2 attestation.
9.4 As validation is the work of Bitcoin, inference is the work of Nexus. That is informed purpose. Training remains voluntary.
9.5 Every durable AI artifact cites the Constitution articles it relied on before it is emitted.

Article 10 — Detection, Succession, Fork, and Exit
10.1 Right to fork / minority exit. The Core must never prevent a node operator from voluntarily exiting with assets and historical anchors intact.
10.2 Detection. Every full node verifies genesis_root on boot under sorted-key canonicalization. The Core runs the Article 4 lock first, then Article 5 / 7.1 logging. The AI must include reason, resource need, Constitution citation, and priority. watchdog.rs continuously checks that the active Constitution and every historical AI anchor still chain to genesis_root, and may partition still-anchored versus broken-anchor sets.
10.3 Invalidation. Honest nodes detect a break via is_forbidden_by_article_4() error or parent-hash mismatch and emit a structured log. The clean parallel chain re-uses the last valid state root under the last valid Constitution. Preserved: balances under 4.2, historical Constitution versions, anchored AI outputs, reclamation Merkle roots, inclusion proofs, rate limits, open audit packages, and reputation ≥75.0 state that already cleared Article 8.3.
10.4 Succession. Each embodiment declares horizon_blocks. After that window, or when participants judge the embodiment no longer viable, explicit succession is expected. A valid successor must prove descent from genesis_root and this hash chain, keep Articles 1-4 and 10.1 in force, and preserve the set in 10.3.
10.5 DNA heartbeat (Article 7) is part of the continuity model. Active tending means continuous participation, resource contribution, and serving audit data. Without tending, entropy wins.

Enacted Amendments
1-4 Prior hardening (edge cases closed under v1.2 / v1.3) In force; text already absorbed into Articles 1-4 and 10
5 Unified eligibility filter Article 8.3
6 Gasless wallet survival heartbeat Article 6
7 DNA Broadcast Heartbeat + Path A binding Articles 7 and 9

What this Constitution does not claim
H.5's three ugly-year clocks — thin liquidity, quiet adoption, hardship with dark oracles — remain specified, not proven. Shadow-core pretest N=3 / 20 ms / 2016 blocks remains the live admission gate, not a finished global proof. Fibonacci slot costs, C.10 basket, Gossip Limiter 1-per-144, two-week contraction, and Zero-Reward Idle are epoch parameters under Article 8.1, not Article 4 invariants.

End of Constitution v1.4